rules_text:
{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last ~5–10 days show a rising regime, the model is systematically too low versus realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply a positive drift add-on that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with forecast length; near-term (days 1–5) is close, but mid/long horizon (days 10–25) is biased low by roughly 1–4 EUR in the examples. Use a ramping bias correction (e.g., +0.1 to +0.2 EUR/day up to a cap).",
    "Lag in capturing breakouts: after a regime shift upward, the forecast trajectory follows the move but with delay, missing the level shift. Use a level-shift detector (e.g., price > recent MA by threshold) to immediately lift the whole forward curve.",
    "Spike/acceleration underreaction: during sharp rallies (multi-day acceleration), the model fails to match the peak magnitude (forecasts are smoother). Add a momentum term proportional to recent returns/volatility to raise peaks in weeks 2–3.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after strong model-led rally: when the model itself projects a steep rise into the far tail, it can overshoot if the true market mean-reverts or reverses near the end of the window. Damp the last ~5–10 forecast days toward a medium-term mean (shrink tail changes)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 7 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger in days 7–25).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the market transitions into an uptrend, the model lags and fails to capture the slope (true series ramps to low-70s while forecast stays high-60s). Add a trend-following term based on the last 5–10 day momentum.",
    "Post-spike behavior: the model anticipates mean reversion after upward breaks (keeps prices near ~68–70) but the true series shows continuation for ~1–2 weeks. Reduce mean-reversion strength after a breakout; favor continuation for the next 5–15 days.",
    "Horizon-specific miscalibration: mid-horizon (≈days 10–22) errors are largest and mostly one-sided (too low). Use a horizon-dependent correction curve (e.g., +0.5 to +1.0 early, +2 to +4 mid-horizon, taper later).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk in some runs: occasionally the model projects excessive late increases (e.g., days 26–30 in Example 2) while the true series flattens/rolls over. Cap late-horizon drift and increase pull toward recent realized level after ~day 20."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most of the 30-day horizon, especially during uptrends/rallies. Apply an upward level shift (positive bias correction), larger in the first ~20 days.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when history transitions into a rising regime, the model increases too slowly and misses the magnitude of the move. Add a momentum/trend-following adjustment (e.g., proportional to recent 5–10d slope) to reduce lag.",
    "Rally peak underestimation: around local highs the model remains several EUR/tCO2 too low (misses the peak amplitude). Use a convex adjustment in strong positive momentum regimes (bigger uplift when recent returns are strongly positive).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: in some cases the model drifts too high at the far end (≈days 25–30) while realized prices mean-revert/lower. Damp long-horizon forecasts toward a mean/anchor (e.g., blend with recent average) to avoid end-of-horizon inflation.",
    "Asymmetric error: model handles down-moves better than up-moves; corrections should be regime-dependent (stronger upward correction in bullish regimes, minimal change in bearish/flat regimes)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Level bias: forecasts are systematically too low during sustained rallies (notably days ~1–20 in all examples). Apply an upward level correction when the last 5–10 days show a positive slope/series of higher highs.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model under-reacts to accelerating uptrends (true series rises faster and peaks higher). Add a momentum term that increases with recent 5–10d return/volatility to reduce lag in trend regimes.",
    "Turning-point error: after the peak, the model stays too high for too long (fails to capture the sharp late-month selloff in Examples 2–3). Introduce stronger mean-reversion / faster decay once prices start making lower lows over 3–5 days.",
    "Horizon asymmetry: early-horizon (≈1–15d) needs upward adjustment in uptrends; late-horizon (≈20–30d) needs downward adjustment when the market transitions to a drawdown. Use regime-switching by recent slope sign and drawdown from recent high.",
    "Oversmoothing of volatility: true outcomes show larger amplitude (higher highs and deeper late declines) than forecasts. Scale forecast deviations from the recent mean by a factor tied to recent realized volatility (i.e., widen moves in high-vol regimes)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing, add a positive level shift to days 1–15 (model is typically ~+0.5 to +2 EUR too low early in the horizon).",
    "Turning-point/peak lag: the model tends to keep forecasting continued strength after a local peak; if spot has just spiked and started to roll over, pull down days 10–30 (reduce slope) because the model reacts too slowly to reversals.",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip: early horizon (≈days 1–12) is biased low during rallies, but late horizon (≈days 20–30) is biased high because the model fails to capture subsequent sell-offs/mean reversion.",
    "Overshoot persistence after spikes: after a sharp up-move, the model projects elevated prices for too long; apply faster mean reversion (compress deviations from the recent mean) beyond ~2 weeks.",
    "Downside tail risk underestimation: when volatility is elevated, the model underestimates the magnitude of late-horizon drawdowns; widen downside adjustment for days 20–30 (more negative skew)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing (e.g., consecutive higher closes / breakout), add an upward bias to the forecast path (model is systematically too low during rallies).",
    "Spike-day lag: after a sharp jump in the true series, the model reacts too slowly and smooths the move; apply an immediate level-shift upward for the first ~7–12 forecast days following a breakout/spike.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: beyond ~day 15–30 the model tends to stay too high versus realized prices (fails to capture subsequent mean reversion/decline); taper forecasts downward with a horizon-increasing negative adjustment.",
    "Turning-point miss: the model often projects persistence at the prevailing level and misses reversals; if recent momentum is weakening (smaller gains / stall after a run-up), enforce stronger mean reversion in weeks 3–4.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast amplitude is too low around large moves (both up and down); widen the response to recent realized volatility, but only in the near term (first ~10 days), then revert to mean-reverting dynamics."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward (model stays ~69–74 while outcomes trend down into mid-60s). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model extrapolates recent strength and fails to capture regime shifts; when the market starts rolling over, forecasts remain elevated for 1–3 weeks. Add a faster mean-reversion component when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term rallies/spikes in history (e.g., move into low 70s), the model projects continued high levels, but outcomes revert and then sell off. Cap post-spike upside and increase reversion speed after large positive moves.",
    "Horizon-dependent error (convex): near-term (days 1–7) errors are smaller; mid/long horizon (days 8–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-weighted correction where the downward adjustment grows with lead time.",
    "Underestimation of downside tail: realized paths show sharper drawdowns (mid-60s/low-60s) than forecast distributions imply. Introduce asymmetric correction: larger negative adjustment when recent volatility rises or when price breaks below recent moving averages."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are generally too high vs realized levels, especially beyond ~day 15–30 (model fails to capture the subsequent downtrend). Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model extrapolates recent strength and reacts too slowly to regime changes; it keeps prices elevated after the market turns lower. Add a rule to detect slope reversal in recent history and dampen/flip the forecasted drift earlier.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term rallies into the low-70s, the model projects continued high/flat prices, but realized prices mean-revert and then sell off. Introduce mean-reversion toward a medium-term anchor (e.g., 20–30d average) when recent prices are >~2–3 EUR above that anchor.",
    "Horizon-dependent error shape: near-term (first ~5–10 days) errors are smaller and sometimes slightly low, but mid/long horizon becomes increasingly overestimated. Use a correction that ramps from ~0 at day 1 to a larger negative adjustment by day 30.",
    "Volatility underestimation on the downside: the model misses the magnitude of drawdowns (realized drops to mid-60s while forecasts stay ~69–74). When recent realized volatility rises or downside momentum appears, widen downside scenarios / apply stronger negative drift."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change/turning-point bias: when the recent history shows a local peak and early signs of rollover, the model keeps projecting elevated/sideways prices; apply a downward correction that grows with horizon (small at days 1–5, materially larger after ~day 10).",
    "Horizon-dependent overestimation: forecasts are slightly low in the first ~1–10 days during the final upswing, then become persistently too high from ~day 15 onward as the realized path mean-reverts lower; implement a piecewise bias adjustment (near-term +0 to +1, mid/long-term −3 to −10).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following sharp run-ups (e.g., into ~71–73), the model underestimates the probability/magnitude of subsequent drawdowns; cap forecast levels relative to the recent 30d high and force faster reversion toward the 30d mean.",
    "Insufficient volatility on the downside: the model’s long-horizon path is too smooth and clustered around ~70–73, while outcomes can slide to low/mid-60s; increase downside tail risk by widening forecast bands and shifting the mean lower when momentum turns negative.",
    "Lag in mean reversion: when the last 30d mean is below the current spot and momentum weakens, pull the forecast toward the 30d mean faster (higher mean-reversion speed), especially beyond day 10."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downtrend/mean-reversion miss: when the recent 30d history shows a peak followed by weakening (lower highs or a sharp drop), the model stays too high and too flat. Apply a downward bias that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 8–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after a short-term rally/spike in history, the model extrapolates elevated levels (often +5 to +10 EUR by weeks 3–4). Damp the forecast by scaling deviations from the last price toward the 30d mean (e.g., shrink forecast changes by ~30–50% beyond day 10).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to regime shifts (it keeps forecasting continuation while reality reverses). If last 5–10 days show momentum reversal (e.g., price up but returns decelerating / or price down but selling pressure accelerating), flip the sign of the model’s incremental trend for the next 1–2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific drift: errors are modest in the first week but become strongly positive bias in weeks 2–4 (model > actual). Introduce a horizon ramp correction: subtract an increasing offset with lead time (e.g., ~0–1 EUR days 1–7, ~2–4 EUR days 8–14, ~5–8 EUR days 15–30, scaled by recent volatility).",
    "Volatility underestimation on sell-offs: true outcomes show sharper declines than forecast during drawdowns. When recent realized volatility is high and downside moves dominate, widen downside moves in the forecast (increase negative daily steps; don’t cap declines)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Downtrend-blindness / bullish bias: when the last ~5–10 days show weakening or a rollover, the model stays too high. Apply a negative level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors are modest in the first week but become strongly positive after ~2 weeks. Use a ramped correction (increasing downward adjustment with forecast step).",
    "Failure to capture regime breaks: after a local peak (~72–73), true prices mean-revert and can transition into a sustained sell-off (~63–66). If recent history includes a peak followed by 2–3 lower closes, force a faster reversion path (reduce slope and lower the terminal level).",
    "Over-smoothing / lag on declines: the model reacts too slowly to emerging bearish momentum. If short-term momentum (e.g., 3–5 day return) is negative, subtract an extra momentum-proportional term from all horizons.",
    "Underestimates drawdown magnitude: during sell-offs the model predicts a mild pullback while reality drops sharply. If volatility has risen and direction is down, widen the downside (increase negative adjustment) rather than keeping forecasts clustered near the prior mean."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change blind spot: when the last 5–10 days show a clear downside break (lower lows / accelerating drawdown), the model keeps forecasting sideways-to-up. Apply a bearish regime switch: shift the entire 30d path down materially and impose a declining slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small to mildly positive, but from ~day 10 onward the model becomes increasingly too high. Apply an increasing negative correction with horizon (e.g., small cut week 1, larger cut weeks 2–4).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a local peak/spike in history, the model extrapolates strength and stays elevated, while realized prices mean-revert sharply lower. Add a post-spike mean-reversion rule: cap upside follow-through and pull forecasts back toward (or below) recent 10–20d average.",
    "Underestimation of drawdown magnitude: during selloffs the model predicts only mild dips and quick recovery; actual outcomes show sustained declines. Increase downside volatility/convexity: allow larger cumulative drops and avoid V-shaped rebounds unless supported by recent upward momentum.",
    "Level anchoring too high: forecasts remain clustered around ~71–73 even as the market transitions to mid-60s. Re-anchor the forecast level to the most recent 3–7 day mean (or last close) rather than the prior month’s higher mean when trend is negative."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: forecasts are consistently too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10; apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Regime-change blindness: when the recent history is flat-to-down, the model still projects stable-to-rising prices; add a trend filter so negative momentum forces a lower path.",
    "Overshoot after local highs/spikes: following a jump in the last observed days, the model extrapolates strength, but outcomes mean-revert and then sell off; dampen post-spike projections and enforce mean reversion toward the recent 20–30d average.",
    "Horizon-dependent divergence: early horizon (days 1–5) errors are small, but mid/late horizon (days 10–30) errors grow rapidly; use a widening correction (e.g., subtract an increasing penalty per forecast day).",
    "Volatility underestimation on the downside: realized paths show sharp drawdowns that the model misses; incorporate a downside stress adjustment when recent volatility is elevated or when price breaks below recent support (e.g., 10–15d low)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miss: after a local peak/spike in history (e.g., ~72–73), the model extrapolates strength, but outcomes mean-revert and often trend down. Add a mean-reversion term pulling forecasts back toward the recent 10–20d average when the last price is above that average.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when the last 1–3 days show an upswing, the model overshoots and keeps prices elevated; realized path typically rolls over within ~5–10 days. Damp recent momentum (reduce slope) and cap upside continuation signals.",
    "Horizon-specific drift: week-1 errors are modest, but weeks 2–4 show growing divergence (model too flat/high while reality declines). Introduce a negative drift component for longer horizons when the recent 30d trend is negative or volatility is elevated.",
    "Volatility underestimation on downside: realized series exhibits sharper drawdowns than forecast. Widen downside adjustment (or apply asymmetric error correction) when recent drawdown/volatility regime is high."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices, with the bias growing with horizon (roughly small in week 1, large by weeks 3–4). Apply a downward level shift that increases with lead time.",
    "Trend/momentum overestimation: the model extrapolates recent strength and predicts continued rises/plateaus, while outcomes mean-revert and often trend down. Damp forecast slopes toward flat/declining paths when the last 2–4 weeks show a run-up.",
    "Failure to capture regime breaks: after local highs/spikes (e.g., ~71–72), the model keeps prices elevated, but realized prices drop sharply. Add an 'overshoot-after-spike' penalty: if recent max is near the end of history, pull the entire forward curve down and increase the pull-down with horizon.",
    "Insufficient mean reversion: realized prices revert toward a lower level (~mid-60s in these samples) much faster than the model implies. Blend forecasts with a mean-reverting anchor (e.g., recent 30d average or a lower regime mean), with higher weight on the anchor at longer horizons.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: days 1–5 need minimal adjustment; days 6–15 require moderate downward correction; days 16–30 require strong downward correction (largest errors occur in the back half of the forecast window)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model systematically overpredicts levels by ~5–10 EUR across most of the 30d horizon, especially after the first week.",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: model extrapolates recent strength upward while realized prices mean-revert and trend down; apply mean-reversion toward recent 30d average rather than continuing the last upswing.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs (~71–73), model projects further gains, but outcomes typically reverse sharply; cap upside and introduce a downward correction immediately after a spike.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast error grows with lead time (small early, large mid/late horizon); increase negative adjustment with horizon (e.g., progressively larger downward shift after day ~7–10).",
    "Volatility misallocation: model keeps prices in a narrow high band while reality breaks lower; allow for regime shift/downside tail risk when recent history shows weakening momentum."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Upward bias needed in rising regimes: when the recent 5–10 day trend turns positive after a drawdown, the model systematically underpredicts the rebound (true path lifts into high-60s while forecast stays mid-60s). Apply a positive drift adjustment that grows with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors are small in days 1–10 but become increasingly positive (true > forecast) from ~day 15 onward. Add a time-increasing uplift (e.g., +0.5 to +1.0 by day 15, +2 to +6 by days 25–30 depending on trend strength).",
    "Lag in capturing momentum: the model reacts too slowly to accelerating upside moves (misses late-horizon breakout to ~68–70). If the last 3–7 days show higher highs/higher lows, shift the forecast trajectory upward earlier (reduce smoothing/lag).",
    "Missed volatility expansion after inflection: after a bottoming pattern, realized upside volatility is higher than forecast (forecast range too tight). Increase forecast variance/step size in the second half of the horizon when a reversal signal is present.",
    "Asymmetry: downside is captured better than upside—model mean-reverts too aggressively on rebounds. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is recovering from a local low (e.g., price below 20–30d average and turning up)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Upward bias correction: the model is systematically too low versus realized prices, especially from ~day 12 onward. Apply an additive uplift that increases with horizon (≈ +0.3 to +0.8 in days 1–10; ≈ +1.0 to +2.5 in days 11–20; ≈ +3.0 to +6.0 in days 21–30).",
    "Trend/lag fix: the model underreacts to emerging uptrends and appears lagged by several days (it forecasts a flatter path while the market grinds higher). Increase sensitivity to recent slope/momentum (e.g., if last 5–10 days trend is positive, tilt the whole forecast path upward).",
    "Late-horizon breakout miss: large errors cluster in the back third of the horizon where the true series rallies strongly but the model stays range-bound. Add a regime rule: if price has rebounded from a local low in the last ~7–14 days, raise days 20–30 materially (convex uplift) rather than keeping mean-reverting levels.",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: forecasts pull back toward mid-60s even when realized prices persistently hold/rise above that level. Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility is low-to-moderate and trend is positive; allow higher plateaus.",
    "Spike handling: after a sharp rebound (e.g., from ~60 to mid-60s), the model tends to fade the move; realized prices continue higher. Replace post-spike ‘fade’ with partial continuation (do not project immediate retracement; keep at least 50–70% of the jump in the level for the next 1–2 weeks)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, the model stays too low (often by ~+2 to +6 €/t) and the gap widens with horizon; apply an upward drift adjustment that increases with lead time.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but mid/late horizon (days ~15–30) systematically undershoots; use a ramped correction (e.g., +0 to +1 early, rising to +3 to +6 by day 30) when trend is positive.",
    "Lag after regime change: the model reacts slowly to breakouts (e.g., move from low-60s to high-60s); if price breaks above recent 10–20d range/high, shift the whole forecast path upward rather than expecting quick mean reversion.",
    "Over-mean-reversion: forecasts pull back toward low/mid-60s even when realized prices hold higher; reduce mean-reversion strength when recent realized volatility is accompanied by higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Spike follow-through missed: after an upside impulse, the model assumes fade, but outcomes show continuation; if last close is >~2 €/t above 10d average, add a continuation premium (increasing with horizon) instead of reverting."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: days 1–10 are close to unbiased to slightly high (model overpredicts small down-moves), but from ~day 15 onward the model becomes strongly bearish vs reality (systematically underpredicts sustained rallies). Apply an increasing positive uplift with horizon (small near-term, larger +€3 to +€7 by days 20–30 in these samples).",
    "Trend/lag error: the model behaves mean-reverting and lags regime shifts. When the last ~5–10 days show a turn up from lows (as in the histories), the true path continues trending higher while the model drifts sideways/down. Add a trend-following component when short-term momentum turns positive.",
    "Rally underestimation after troughs: after a local bottom (e.g., ~60–62 in history), the model forecasts only a mild rebound, but outcomes show a stronger, persistent climb into the high 60s. If recent min is within last ~10 days and price has rebounded, scale up rebound magnitude and persistence.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s forecast range is too narrow; it misses the amplitude of late-horizon moves (true outcomes reach ~68–70 while forecasts stay ~62–66). Widen forecast distribution/levels upward in bullish regimes rather than pulling back to the mean.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot-to-mean: the model repeatedly pulls prices back toward ~64–66 even when realized prices break out above that zone. If price breaks above recent resistance (~66–67 here), reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 15 onward; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the market transitions into an upswing, the model reacts too slowly (lag) and misses the slope; add a positive trend term when recent returns turn positive.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small/mixed, but medium-to-long horizon (days 10–30) increasingly underpredicts; use a piecewise correction (small early, larger later).",
    "Rally phase under-capture: after a local trough in history, the model mean-reverts too strongly and fails to project sustained rebound; reduce mean-reversion strength when last-30d momentum is positive or when price is rising off lows.",
    "Peak underestimation rather than overshoot: during strong rallies the model does not overshoot; it caps highs too low—raise upper tail/volatility scaling for longer horizons in trending regimes."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent upside bias in realized prices vs model: forecasts are systematically too low, especially from ~day 10 onward when the market trends up (model misses sustained rallies). Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small/mixed, but medium/long horizon (days ~10–30) shows large positive forecast errors (true > forecast). Use a ramping correction (e.g., +0.2 to +0.5 €/t early, rising to +2 to +5 €/t by day 20–30 in strong uptrends).",
    "Trend/lag effect: model behaves like a lagged mean-reversion process—after a turning point upward, it adjusts too slowly. Add a momentum term based on recent 5–10 day slope; when slope is positive, boost all horizons, with larger boosts further out.",
    "Underreaction to volatility spikes upward: when the true series accelerates (e.g., mid/late horizon jump to high-60s), the model caps/plateaus in mid-60s. Add convexity: if recent returns are large positive, increase forecast variance and shift the mean upward (avoid flattening).",
    "Asymmetry: model is closer during declines/flat periods but materially underpredicts during upswings. Use regime switching—apply stronger upward correction only when recent momentum/price breaks above recent range (e.g., above 10–20d high)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the market transitions from a dip to a sustained rally, the model follows too slowly and underestimates the slope. Add a momentum/trend term based on the last 5–10 days’ return to steepen the forecast path.",
    "Underestimation of upside after breakouts: once prices clear the recent range (new local highs), realized prices continue higher while the model caps/plateaus. Use a regime switch: if price > max(last 10–15 days), boost subsequent 10–30d forecasts by an additional premium.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors compound with time (near-term roughly calibrated; long-horizon increasingly too low). Implement a linear (or piecewise) horizon scaling of the correction rather than a constant offset.",
    "Volatility underpricing in rallies: model path is too smooth during uptrends and misses higher peaks. Inflate variance/upper tail (e.g., widen forecast distribution or add convex uplift) conditional on positive recent momentum."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: forecasts are systematically too low vs outcomes, especially from ~day 5 onward; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: model underreacts to sustained uptrends; when recent history shows a rebound or rising slope, increase the forecast slope (add a positive drift term) rather than mean-reverting back down.",
    "Underestimation of rallies/spikes: during strong upside moves the model caps/plateaus while reality continues higher; introduce a momentum kicker when last 5–10 day return is strongly positive (scale up forecasts more at mid/long horizons).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors widen materially with lead time; use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., additive premium increasing roughly linearly from near-zero at day 1 to several €/t by day 30).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is too smooth (lower amplitude) vs realized; expand deviations from the starting level (increase variance) in the direction of the prevailing trend, particularly after upside breaks."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: model under-forecasts levels throughout the horizon, especially during rallies. Apply an upward level shift (roughly +1 to +4 €/t early, rising to +3 to +6 €/t mid/late horizon when trend is up).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: model reacts too slowly to upward momentum; true series ramps faster. Add a momentum term based on recent 5–10d slope (if slope > 0, boost forecast increasingly with horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors grow with lead time (small at days 1–3, larger from ~day 7 onward). Use a lead-time scaling factor on the correction (e.g., minimal adjustment near-term, larger adjustment mid/long-term).",
    "Volatility compression: model forecasts are too smooth and miss breakout amplitude. Increase forecast variance during strong-trend regimes (e.g., when last 10d range or ATR is elevated).",
    "Regime misclassification after local dips: when history shows a drawdown then stabilization, model assumes continued weakness/mean reversion, but true outcomes often reverse into a sustained uptrend. If price is below recent 10–20d average but slope turns positive, flip to bullish correction rather than mean-reversion down."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong negative bias across the whole 30D horizon: model systematically underpredicts levels (true outcomes are consistently higher). Apply an upward level shift to forecasts.",
    "Uptrend/acceleration miss: when the recent history is turning up, the model lags the rally and fails to capture momentum; increase sensitivity to recent positive returns (trend-following adjustment) especially in days ~5–25.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is too smooth and too low-amplitude versus realized moves. Expand deviations from the starting point (scale forecast changes up) during trending regimes.",
    "Horizon-specific bias grows with lead time: underprediction is small at very short horizons but widens materially mid/late horizon. Use a lead-time-dependent uplift (larger additive correction for longer horizons).",
    "Regime shift blindness: after a local low in history, the model continues to mean-revert downward while the market transitions to a higher-price regime. Add a regime-detection rule (e.g., if last 5–10D slope > 0 and price above short MA, reduce mean-reversion and apply upward drift)."
  ]
}

{
